<section id="section-faq" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={`${prose} space-y-6`}>
    <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">1. How many days do you need in Paris?</h3>
      <p>Three days is a good amount of time for a first visit if you want to see the main sights. But if you also want to include a day trip, such as Versailles or Disneyland Paris, or visit some additional museums, I'd say four to five days is a better option.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">2. What is the best time to visit Paris?</h3>
      <p>Spring and fall are great choices, especially April to June and September to October, when the weather is generally pleasant and crowds can be lighter than during the summer. That said, Paris is a year-round destination, and each season has something different to offer.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">3. Is Paris expensive?</h3>
      <p>Paris can be expensive, especially when it comes to hotels and eating out, but you don't have to spend a fortune to enjoy the city. There are plenty of free things to do, and you can save money by eating at bakeries and casual cafés, using public transportation and choosing accommodation outside the most expensive areas.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">4. Is Paris easy to explore on foot?</h3>
      <p>Yes, especially if you enjoy walking. One of my favorite walks I've done in Paris was from the Louvre all the way to the Arc de Triomphe. It was such a lovely route because I could take my time, admire the beautiful buildings along the way and simply enjoy the walk.</p>
      <p>I would definitely recommend it if you enjoy exploring, want to explore the city at a slower pace and don't like to pack your itinerary with too many things, trying to see everything in just a few days.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">5. Do you need to book attractions in advance?</h3>
      <p>For some of the most popular attractions, I definitely recommend it. The Eiffel Tower and Louvre can get very busy, especially during peak travel periods, so booking ahead can save you from long waits or finding that your preferred time is sold out.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">6. Do you need to speak French in Paris?</h3>
      <p>Not really. Paris is such an international city that you'll find plenty of people who speak English, especially in hotels, restaurants and tourist areas. So, don't worry if you don't speak French. But you can always say "bonjour" when you enter a shop or restaurant. It's simply considered polite.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-14" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Before you go">
      <p>Paris is a city where you can easily fill every minute of your itinerary, but I wouldn't recommend trying to see everything in one trip. The best advice I can give you is to leave some time to simply walk around, stop for a coffee or a pastry, sit somewhere and watch the city for a while. And I'm sure some of your favorite moments in Paris will be the ones you never expected!</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
